 ## Overview
-This method computes perspective-correct splat shapes for point-based rendering by approximating the projective mapping of Gaussian reconstruction kernels from their local tangent planes to image space using affine mappings derived from conic isocontours [S2, unofficial]. Unlike previous methods that used affine approximations exact only at the kernel center, this approach ensures accuracy at the kernel cutoff boundary, preventing artifacts such as holes and incorrect elongation under extreme perspective projections [S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem where previous projections integrated kernels along the z-axis rather than the oblique ray from the camera, causing particles to be too faint by $\cos(\theta)$ and insufficiently elongated radially [S1].
+This method computes perspective-correct splat shapes for point-based rendering by approximating the projective mapping of Gaussian reconstruction kernels from their local tangent planes to image space using affine mappings derived from conic isocontours [S2, unofficial]. Unlike previous EWA splatting methods that used affine approximations exact only at the kernel center, this approach ensures accuracy at the kernel cutoff boundary, preventing holes and artifacts under extreme perspective projections [S2, unofficial]. It builds on the [Centralized Conics Approach](1243-centralized-conics-approach.md) by providing the specific mechanism for deriving the correct variance matrix in screen space.
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- A set of point samples $\mathbf{p}_k$ with associated color $f_k$ and local tangent frames defined by tangent vectors $\mathbf{t}_u, \mathbf{t}_v$ [S2, unofficial].
-- A Gaussian reconstruction kernel with a user-specified cutoff value $F_g$ (typically $1 < F_g < 2$) [S2, unofficial].
+- A set of point samples $p_k$ with associated colors $f_k$ and local tangent frames defined by tangent vectors $\mathbf{t}_u, \mathbf{t}_v$ [S2, unofficial].
+- A Gaussian reconstruction kernel $g$ with a user-specified cutoff value $F_g$ (typically $1 < F_g < 2$) [S2, unofficial].
 - Camera projection parameters.
 
 **Outputs:**
-- Screen-space rasterization of splats with correct perspective shape and intensity [S2, unofficial].
+- Screen-space splat accumulation (color and weight) for each pixel.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-- **Tangent Plane Matrix ($M_k$):** A $3 \times 3$ matrix mapping local tangent coordinates $(u, v)$ to 3D world space, formed by rows $\mathbf{t}_u$, $\mathbf{t}_v$, and $\mathbf{p}_k$ [S2, unofficial]. Points $\mathbf{p}$ on the plane are expressed as $\mathbf{p} = (u, v, 1) M_k$ [S2, unofficial].
-- **Conic Matrix ($Q_h$):** A $3 \times 3$ symmetric matrix representing the implicit form of a general conic in homogeneous coordinates: $Q_h = \begin{bmatrix} A & B & D \\ B & C & E \\ D & E & -F \end{bmatrix}$ [S2, unofficial]. The conic equation is $[x \ y \ 1] Q_h [x \ y \ 1]^T = 0$ [S2, unofficial].
-- **Variance Matrix ($R'_k$):** The covariance matrix of the reconstruction kernel in the tangent plane [S2, unofficial].
+- **Tangent Frame Matrix ($M_k$):** A $3 \times 3$ matrix mapping local 2D coordinates $(u, v)$ to 3D world space.
+  $$ \mathbf{M}_k = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{t}_u \\ \mathbf{t}_v \\ \mathbf{p}_k \end{bmatrix} $$
+  Points $\mathbf{p}$ on the tangent plane are expressed as $\mathbf{p} = (u, v, 1) \mathbf{M}_k$ [S2, unofficial].
+- **Conic Matrix ($Q_h$):** A $3 \times 3$ symmetric matrix representing the implicit form of a conic $\phi(x, y) = Ax^2 + 2Bxy + Cy^2 + 2Dx + 2Ey - F = 0$ in homogeneous coordinates:
+  $$ Q_h = \begin{bmatrix} A & B & D \\ B & C & E \\ D & E & -F \end{bmatrix} $$
+  The equation is $\mathbf{x} Q_h \mathbf{x}^T = 0$ where $\mathbf{x} = [x, y, 1]$ [S2, unofficial].
+- **Variance Matrix ($R_k$):** The $2 \times 2$ covariance matrix of the Gaussian kernel in the local tangent frame.
+- **Mapped Variance Matrix ($Q'''$):** The $2 \times 2$ variance matrix of the reconstruction kernel in image space, derived from the projective mapping [S2, unofficial].
 
